diff --git a/docs/task-agent-discovery.md b/docs/task-agent-discovery.md
index be4b8ea371..c0c9109fbb 100644
--- a/docs/task-agent-discovery.md
+++ b/docs/task-agent-discovery.md
@@ -296,6 +296,8 @@ Automatic selection applies to every spawn that carries no model of any kind (no
 
 The native judge needs the `judge` role to resolve first (after its own configured fallback chain and the credentialed models) to a model whose API is a native judgment API: TypeSafe (`typesafe/jev-latest` with `TYPESAFE_API_KEY`), OpenRouter Decisions, Workers AI or OpenAI Decisions. A judge role that resolves to an ordinary chat model is not a native judge.
 
+Automatic selection never picks a model that bills real money. Having an alias in `modelRoles` does not name it for this call, so an alias whose model bills real money is not offered to the judge; the warning on the result, the subagent row and the notice name each alias that was left out. When every alias is left out the spawn fails before the child starts and says that each one bills real money. A model bills real money unless its provider is signed in with OAuth, the catalogue lists it as free or included, it runs on this machine, or the provider's usage endpoint reports plan limit windows for your account; what that evidence cannot decide counts as billable. A model named for the call (a per-call `@role`, a `task.agentModelOverrides` entry, an agent's `model`) is always allowed. The same rule keeps billable models out of the judge's role chain and its session-model fallback, and out of the built-in defaults of helper roles (`smol`, `tiny`, `memory`, `widget`, `advisor`), the `image`, `speech`, `dictation` and `web` roles, the compaction fallback model, the eval `completion()` tiers and the main session's startup model (with no default, or a default that cannot be used); a role you configured yourself keeps its model, and so does a `--model` or a default you set. A helper that ends up with no model for this reason shows one notice per session; a session that cannot start says which models were left out and to choose one with `/model` or `--model`.
+
 After automatic selection, the `before_subagent_spawn` extension hook runs once for the actual dispatch. It can block any spawn, or replace model patterns when no host or per-call model was supplied; a replacement and its routing note are reported. An explicit host or per-call selector cannot be replaced by the hook. A subagent has no runtime fallback model: the only chain it keeps is one you wrote in `retry.fallbackChains` under the exact `provider/model-id` it runs on. Using that chain raises the same visible warning, and a result whose child ended on another model than it started on says so in its `<model-warning>`. Task results report the requested and actual model.
 
 The `Alt+P` task model pick is session-only; saving a model in `/agents` replaces that runtime selection for the current session and persists the new value for future sessions. These agent overrides apply when automatic selection does not pick a usable role.
diff --git a/docs/tools/task.md b/docs/tools/task.md
index 7c0e6aea3a..76c98d7ea9 100644
--- a/docs/tools/task.md
+++ b/docs/tools/task.md
@@ -70,6 +70,8 @@ When per-call selection is enabled for a surface, the task/eval tool description
 
 **Price tiers.** A task item's `model` may also be `$`, `$$`, `$$$` or `$$$$` (task calls only; eval calls need an exact selector). The tiers are quarters of the blended price (input plus output per million tokens) across every loaded chat model that has a price, so a tier means the same thing whichever providers you are signed in to. The scale is set by the whole loaded catalogue; only the role choice is limited to roles you configured. A model with no price (free tiers, subscriptions, local models) cannot be placed on a price scale, so it takes no tier and is never picked by one. A tier resolves to the configured role in that tier, or in the nearest tier when there is none (the cheaper side wins a tie), and from there it is approved, dispatched and revived exactly like that role. Within a tier the most used role wins. Usage is the share of the last 90 days of requests in `stats.db` (read-only, from the hourly rollup), weighted by how specific the match is: the exact `provider/model-id` counts 0.6, the same model family 0.3 and the same provider 0.1. With no history the order falls back to the role name. The task description shows each configured role's tier, tells agents to use the cheapest tier that does the work reliably, and calls `$$$$` very rare. The judge that picks a role for a call without `model` is shown the same tiers and usage shares.
 
+**Plans, not dollars.** A tier is a choice omp makes, so it never resolves to a role whose model bills real money. A model does not bill real money when its provider is signed in with OAuth (a subscription), the catalogue lists it as free or included, it runs on this machine, or the provider's own usage endpoint reports plan limit windows (5 hour, weekly, monthly) for your account, as opencode-go and the token plans do on an API key. A provider with no usage endpoint, an endpoint that is down, or one that reports only a balance is undecided, and undecided counts as billable. A billable role is left out with a notice that names it; if no other role has a known price the call fails and says so. Name the role itself (`model: "@role"`) to use it anyway.
+
 ### Changing a running subagent's model
 
 `write proc://<agentId>/model` with a configured role (`@haiku`), a price tier (`$` to `$$$$`, resolved exactly as for a task `model`) or one exact `provider/model-id[:level]` as `content` switches a running subagent that the caller spawned (the task description tells the agent to use it when the user names a model or tier for a running agent, for example "use haiku for RefactorWork"). The id is the agent's name or the id of the job running it. The call never aborts or restarts the agent: it finishes the request in flight on its current model, and its next model request uses the new one, with its context, tools and turn intact. The change is session-only (a `temporary` model change in the agent's transcript, plus a notice in its view when a queued switch lands) and is not persisted to settings or roles. An agent between requests switches immediately; a switch queued while a request is in flight is applied at the next request boundary and the newest one wins. A per-call worker's saved identity follows the switch, so a revival restores it on the model it last ran on.
diff --git a/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md b/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md
index 1854ed904e..886553fa4c 100644
--- a/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -26,6 +26,8 @@
 - `task.prewalk`, `task.agentPrewalk` and an agent's `prewalk` frontmatter no longer apply to subagents: a subagent never hands off to another model. Session-level prewalk is unchanged.
 - `omp update` and the startup update check now use the latest stable release of the fork's GitHub repository (`kierandrewett/omp`, versions like `18.14.2-fork.1`): the `omp-linux-x64` asset is checked against `SHA256SUMS.txt` before it replaces the running binary, and the previous binary is restored if the new one fails version verification. `PI_BUILD_URL` and the upstream npm registry are no longer consulted, and npm, bun, Homebrew, mise and Nix installs are not updated from here.
 
+- Automatic model selection never picks a model that bills real money. That covers the subagent classifier, the price tiers `$` to `$$$$`, the judge (its role chain and the session-model fallback) and the helper roles (titles, memory, widgets, commit messages, skill descriptions, the vision fallback, advisors), the unconfigured image, speech, dictation and web roles, the compaction fallback model, the eval completion tiers, and the main session's startup model when no default is configured or the configured one is unavailable. A helper left without a model for this reason tells you once per session (a notice), and a session with no model to start on says which models were left out and how to choose one. A model counts as free of real money when its provider is signed in with OAuth, the catalogue lists it as free or included, it runs on this machine, or the provider's own usage endpoint reports plan limit windows for your account (opencode-go and other flat-rate plans on an API key). Anything the evidence cannot decide is billable. A billable alias is left out with a visible note that names it, and when every alias is left out the spawn fails with a message that says so. A model you name yourself (a per-call `@role`, `task.agentModelOverrides`, an agent's `model`, or a role you configured for that helper) is still used.
+
 ### Fixed
 
 - Fixed a lone `.` or `c` typed into a focused subagent that was still running, or waiting out a retry back-off, printing `Agent is already processing` as an error. The shortcut now queues as a steering message like any other text; the continue shortcut itself only restarts an idle agent.
